  :text
  ";; The capability grant the Kotoba compiler (amu) holds when it runs as a\n;; program -- the abilities its own modules name (superproject\n;; adr-2609242330 S5). scripts/measure-selfhost-distance.cljk passes this as\n;; `--policy` on the project route, so a compiler module that names an\n;; ability is admitted with the authority the compiler actually has, and a\n;; refusal on the scoreboard is a language wall rather than \"no grant was\n;; given to a measurement\".\n;;\n;; Every entry is a catalog capability (lang/capability-catalog.edn) with a\n;; handler in the nbb mechanism layer (amu src/kotoba/compiler/nbb/host/).\n;; All but hash/sha256 also have a Linux static ELF handler (adr-2609251400\n;; C3). hash/sha256 is answered by the kexe loader (wire 3) and has no C3\n;; handler yet; the pure org-nist-sha2 computes the same value, and amu\n;; test/nbb/host_abilities.cljk pins that the nbb handler agrees with it.\n;; process/spawn and entropy/draw join when a compiler module names them.\n;;\n;; Twelve entries (2026-09-25, S5 second half). The eight-grant limit was\n;; an engine bug -- cljs.core/hash had no case for a BigInt, and a set hashes\n;; from its ninth element on -- fixed by org-babashka-nbb 829f0ba1 (amu\n;; #1143). The four added are what the migrated modules name: fs/browse 34\n;; (verdict_cache, package_lock, fs_tree), process/spawn 20 (classpath,\n;; package_lock, package_authoring, test_profile), entropy/draw 23\n;; (verdict_cache) and sys/cwd 40 (verdict_cache). The nbb mechanism layer\n;; answers all four; sys/cwd has no C3 handler yet.\n{:allow #{[:cap/call :cli/args]\n          [:cap/call :clock/now]\n          [:cap/call :entropy/draw]\n          [:cap/call :env/read]\n          [:cap/call :fs/app-data]\n          [:cap/call :fs/browse]\n          [:cap/call :hash/sha256]\n          [:cap/call :io/read]\n          [:cap/call :io/write]\n          [:cap/call :io/write-error]\n          [:cap/call :process/spawn]\n          [:cap/call :sys/cwd]}}\n"},
